      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/eggroll20xd6/{size}/1993_2.png",
      "created_at": "2016-02-29T21:02:44.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"jffdougan\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/jffdougan/48/1882_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> jffdougan:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div><b>Trajector wrote:</b><br><blockquote>&nbsp;Belle Sorciere wrote:<br><blockquote>People seem to be anxious about the end of SotM, from commentary on this forum as well as elsewhere. I think what they're looking for is reassurance.&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote>\nI think two things will strongly affect people's opinions:1. How Spirit Island does in game stores.\n</blockquote></div>\nThis, especially, I think. Neither GSF nor Sentinel Tactics has met with the same kind of overwhelming reception as SotM did.\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>I feel that if people actually knew how to play the two games or create scenarios for Tactics, both would be just as popular as SotM. The biggest hurdle GTG has is their success as it seems they offered Sentinels products with each of those campaigns. My guess is that most played the games once because they are so familiar with the core game. I hope both see a revitalization as both are good in their own rights</p>",
